## Disabled Band Reduction Scheme

If your home has been **adapted or has extra space** because of a disabled person who lives there, your bill may be charged at **one band lower**. (If your home is already in the lowest band, you get a reduction instead.) This can apply if your home has:

- an **extra bathroom or kitchen** needed by the disabled person
- a room, other than a bathroom, kitchen or toilet, used mainly for the disabled person's needs, such as for therapy or equipment
- **extra space** for using a **wheelchair indoors**

It does not depend on income. Apply to your council.

## Severe mental impairment

People with a **severe mental impairment**, such as **dementia**, a severe **learning disability** or a severe brain injury, can be **disregarded** for council tax. This means:

- if they live **alone**, or only with others who are also disregarded, the home may be **exempt** from council tax
- if they live with **one other adult**, the bill can be reduced by **25%**

They need a **doctor's certificate** and to get a qualifying benefit, such as PIP daily living, DLA care (middle or higher rate), Attendance Allowance or ESA.

## Carers

Some **live-in carers** can be disregarded, which may reduce the bill, if they care for at least 35 hours a week and are not the person's partner or the parent of a child under 18.

## Council Tax Reduction

If you are on a **low income** or get benefits, you may get **Council Tax Reduction** (sometimes called council tax support). Each council runs its own scheme in England. Apply to your council.

## Backdating

Many councils will **backdate** these discounts, sometimes by several years, if you were eligible but did not claim. Ask.
